course: everyday
lesson: 29
title: Wild birds, bats and germs from animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Animals can carry germs and tiny parasites that do them little harm but can make people ill: salmonella on turtles, lizards and baby chicks, worms in dog poo, Lyme disease from ticks, and rabies or similar viruses in some bats.
explain: The simplest protection is washing your hands with soap and warm water for about 20 seconds after touching animals, their cages, food or poo, and before you eat.
explain: Never touch a bat, even one lying on the ground, or a dead bird or animal: tell an adult, who will call a wildlife service or rescue.
explain: After walking in long grass or woods, check your skin for ticks; if you find one, an adult removes it with fine tweezers, pulling it gently straight out, close to the skin.
explain: Wild birds and their nests deserve respect: watch them from a distance, never disturb a nest or take eggs, and in many countries nests of wild birds are protected by law.
explain: For example, a boy who holds his pet tortoise and then eats crisps without washing his hands can swallow salmonella germs and get a bad stomach illness.
explain: Children who visited a petting farm in England and did not wash their hands properly caught a germ called E. coli; some became very ill and had to go to hospital.
explain: A boy in California picked up a bat lying on the ground; it bit him, and he needed a series of injections to protect him from rabies.
fact: Animals can carry germs that make people ill even when the animals look healthy.
fact: Turtles, tortoises, lizards, snakes and baby chicks often carry salmonella germs.
fact: Wash your hands with soap and water for about 20 seconds after touching animals and before eating.
fact: Never touch a bat; some bats carry rabies or similar viruses.
fact: Any bite or scratch from a bat or a wild animal must be shown to a doctor quickly.
fact: Never touch a dead bird or dead wild animal with bare hands.
fact: Ticks are tiny biters that live in long grass and woods and can spread Lyme disease.
fact: Check your skin for ticks after walks in long grass or woods.
fact: Dog poo can contain worm eggs, so it should always be picked up and binned.
fact: Worms are common in children and are easily treated with medicine from a doctor or chemist.
fact: It is against the law in many countries to disturb the nests of wild birds or take their eggs.
fact: Hand gel does not clean well when hands are visibly dirty, and after touching farm animals soap and water work better than gel.
fact: Soap loosens dirt and germs so that water can rinse them away; water alone washes off much less.
fact: Bats can carry rabies, a disease that is deadly once symptoms start but can be prevented with injections given soon after a bite.
fact: Animal germs can make people very ill, so hands must be washed with soap and water after touching animals.
exercise: Which pets often carry salmonella germs? | Turtles, lizards and baby chicks | Only wild animals, never pets | Cats only | No pets ever carry germs, only farm animals
exercise: When should you wash your hands? | After touching animals and before eating | Only after going swimming in the pool | Only once a week | Only when they look dirty
exercise: How long should you scrub your hands with soap? | About 20 seconds | About 1 second only | About 10 whole minutes | You do not need soap at all
exercise: A bat is lying on the ground in the playground. What should you do? | Not touch it and tell an adult | Pick it up and take it home | Poke it to see if it can fly | Put it in your lunch box to keep it safe
exercise: Why should you never touch a bat? | Some bats carry rabies or similar viruses | Bats are made of glass | Bats can sting like bees | Bats turn into birds when they are touched by people
exercise: Where do ticks usually live? | In long grass and woods | In clean, cold swimming pools | Out in deep snow in winter | Only ever inside people's houses
exercise: What disease can ticks spread? | Lyme disease | Chickenpox | A cold | Tooth decay and gum disease
exercise: What should you do after a walk through long grass? | Check your skin for ticks | Have a big snack straight away | Go straight to sleep | Nothing, ticks do not exist
exercise: Why should dog owners always pick up dog poo? | It can contain worm eggs that make people ill | It makes the grass grow too fast and too long | It is too heavy for the grass | Dogs like it to be taken away
exercise: You find a dead bird in the garden. What should you do? | Leave it alone and tell an adult | Pick it up with your bare hands | Bury it with your hands | Take it to school to show your friends
exercise: You find a bird's nest with eggs in a hedge. What is right? | Leave it alone and watch from far away | Take one egg home to hatch | Move the nest to a safer place | Touch the eggs gently to check that they are warm
exercise: Which works best on hands that are visibly dirty? | Soap and water | A bit of hand gel | Wiping on your clothes | Blowing on them
exercise: Why can an animal that looks healthy still make you ill? | It can carry germs that do not harm it | Healthy animals are always the most dangerous | Germs only live on sick animals | Looking healthy makes germs grow
exercise: A wild fox scratches your hand. What should happen? | Wash it and see a doctor quickly | Just put a plaster on it | Lick it clean | Wait a week to see if it starts to hurt
test: Ben in Ontario holds his pet turtle and then eats an apple without washing. What might happen? | He could swallow salmonella germs and get ill | Nothing, pet turtles never carry any germs at all | The turtle will catch his cold | The apple will turn green
test: Which is true? | A healthy-looking animal can still carry germs | Only sick animals carry germs | Germs cannot pass from animals to people | Washing hands with water alone kills all germs
test: A girl in Texas sees a bat flopping on the porch in the daytime. Which plan is safest? | Keep away and get an adult to call for help | Wrap it in a towel and hold it | Feed it some fruit | Pick it up gently to help it fly back to its home
test: Ellie in New Hampshire finds a tiny tick stuck on her leg after camping. What should happen? | An adult removes it with fine tweezers, pulling straight out | She squashes it on her skin with her thumb until it lets go | She burns it off with a match | She leaves it for a week
test: Why do parks ask dog owners to pick up after their dogs? | Dog poo can spread worm eggs to children | Dog poo is bad luck | It makes the park smell nicer for dogs | It feeds the birds
test: Children find a blackbird's nest in a garden in Glasgow. Why should they not take an egg? | It harms the bird's family and is against the law | The eggs are always empty | Blackbirds do not care about their eggs, they lay more | Eggs hatch faster at home
test: Why does washing with soap work better than just water? | Soap loosens the dirt and germs so they rinse away | Water makes germs grow faster | Soap smells nice to germs, so they come out to eat it | Water alone is always dirty
test: A boy is bitten by a wild animal in the woods but the bite is small. What is the right step? | Wash it and see a doctor quickly | Ignore it because it is small | Cover it and tell nobody | Wait until it gets infected, then see a doctor
test: Which is the best time to wash your hands at a farm or zoo? | After touching the animals and before eating | Only when you get home the next day | Before touching the animals only | Washing is never needed at a zoo
test: bat on the ground, poor thing, pick it up? | No, never touch a bat | Yes, with bare hands gently | Yes, if it looks tired | Take it home in a shoe box
test: back from long grass, what do i check for | Ticks on your skin | Splinters in your shoes | Grass seeds in your hair | Nothing at all, you're fine
test: tortoise looks clean, why wash hands | Reptiles can carry salmonella | They leave dust on you | Tortoise shells are sharp | It's only for dogs
test: dead bird in the garden, can i pick it up | Not with bare hands | Yes, it can't hurt you | Yes, if it looks clean | Yes, give it a big hug
test: found a bat on the ground, it's not moving, can i pick it up | No, do not touch it and tell an adult | Yes, with your bare hands carefully | Yes, put it in your pocket | Yes, if it looks hurt
test: just fed the goats at the farm, can i eat my sandwich now | Wash your hands with soap and water first | Wipe your hands on your trousers until they're clean | Just eat with the other hand | Lick your fingers clean
test: Jack says hand gel is just as good as washing after the petting zoo. true? | No, soap and water get rid of farm germs better | Yes, gel kills everything | Yes, washing is pointless | Yes, gel works best on muddy hands
test: did u hear a kid got rabies from a bat in his bedroom and he didn't even know it bit him | Real lesson: never touch bats, and any contact with a bat must be seen by a doctor quickly | Real lesson: bats are only in bedrooms at night | It proves all bats in every country have rabies | It is gossip, so bats are safe to handle
test: my mate said turtles are clean cos they live in water so u don't need to wash ur hands | Wrong; turtles often carry salmonella, so wash your hands after touching them | He is right, water keeps them germ-free | He is right, only furry pets carry germs | He is right, unless the turtle is ill
test: Owen had worms and someone told the whole class, now nobody sits with him | Do not spread it; worms are common and easily treated, and the gossip hurts him | Spread it, so everyone stays safe | Spread it, because it is true | Spread it, and say he caught them from his dog
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the carrying of germs by animals? | Animals may carry germs harmful to people even whilst appearing healthy | Only animals that appear ill carry germs | Germs from animals cannot affect people | Only wild animals ever carry germs
exam: Identify the recommended duration of hand washing with soap and water after contact with animals. | Approximately twenty seconds | Approximately two seconds | Approximately five minutes | Hand washing is unnecessary after contact
exam: It being given that a walk has been taken through long grass or woodland, select the precaution consistent with established guidance. | The skin should be examined for ticks | The clothes should be shaken in the long grass | The legs should be washed in a stream | No precaution is required
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning a dead wild bird found in a garden. | It must not be touched with bare hands | It may be handled freely if it appears clean | It should be buried at once with the hands | It may be picked up if it has been dead for a day
